// Day 26 — where the time actually goes, per dependency.
//
// ORDERED BY TOTAL TIME, NOT BY p99, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE QUERY.
//
// The instinct is to sort by the slowest call and start there. That finds the
// wrong target more often than not: a 4 ms query executed 12,000 times costs
// 48 seconds of wall clock, while a 900 ms call made twice costs 1.8. Sorting
// by p99 puts the second at the top and the first nowhere. totalMs is the
// latency budget — it is what you are actually spending — so it is what this
// sorts on, with the percentiles kept alongside because they answer the
// different question of whether any single call is pathological.
//
// DependencyType separates the kinds without needing to know the targets:
//   SQL            — EF Core, via AddEntityFrameworkCoreInstrumentation
//   Azure Service Bus — publish and receive, from the Azure SDK's own source
//   HTTP           — outbound calls, from the distro's HttpClient instrumentation
//
// A dependency type MISSING from these results is a finding, not an absence.
// If SQL never appears, EF Core instrumentation is not registered; if Service
// Bus never appears, the publisher is the NoOp implementation and no message
// is being sent at all. An empty row is evidence about the wiring.
AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| summarize
    calls    = sum(ItemCount),
    totalMs  = sum(DurationMs),
    p50      = round(percentile(DurationMs, 50), 1),
    p99      = round(percentile(DurationMs, 99), 1),
    failures = sumif(ItemCount, Success == false)
    by DependencyType, Target, Name
| extend
    totalSeconds  = round(totalMs / 1000.0, 2),
    msPerCall     = round(totalMs / calls, 1),
    failureRatePct = round(100.0 * failures / calls, 2)
| project DependencyType, Target, Name, calls, totalSeconds, msPerCall, p50, p99, failures, failureRatePct
| order by totalMs desc
